# Quiet shell directives

    :b sh cat echo (available)

## Successful setup still runs

Both directive spellings and both quiet flags retain exit annotations but hide
successful stdout and stderr. The following read proves setup actually ran.

    :$ -q sh -c "printf 'created\n' > quiet.txt; printf 'hidden stdout\n'; printf 'hidden stderr\n' >&2" (exit: 0)
    :shell --quiet cat quiet.txt (exit: 0)
    :$ cat quiet.txt (exit: 0)

```stdout
created
```

    :shell -q echo hidden short flag (exit: 0)
    :$ --quiet echo hidden long flag (exit: 0)

Successful ANSI output is also hidden, without output-derived SVGs.

    :shell -q sh -c "printf '\033[31mhidden ansi\033[0m\n'" (exit: 0)

## Executable flags are not directive flags

Flags after the executable remain arguments even when they look like directive
flags. Non-quiet commands keep their output.

    :shell echo -q --quiet --help (exit: 0)

```stdout
-q --quiet --help
```

    :$ sh -c 'printf "%s\n" "$@"' args -q --quiet (exit: 0)

```stdout
-q
--quiet
```

    :shell -q sh -c 'printf "%s\n" "$@" > args.txt' args -q --quiet --help (exit: 0)
    :$ cat args.txt (exit: 0)

```stdout
-q
--quiet
--help
```

## Failures remain visible

Unsuccessful quiet commands show both captured streams. Signal termination also
counts as failure.

    :$ -q sh -c "printf 'failure stdout\n'; printf 'failure stderr\n' >&2; exit 7" (exit: 7)

```stdout
failure stdout
```

```stderr
failure stderr
```

    :shell --quiet sh -c "printf 'killed stdout\n'; printf 'killed stderr\n' >&2; kill -TERM $$$$" (exit: killed)

```stdout
killed stdout
```

```stderr
killed stderr
```

Missing executables still produce spawn diagnostics.

    :shell -q missing-quiet-command (failed)

> [!WARNING]
> failed to execute command: No such file or directory (os error 2)

    :$ --quiet missing-quiet-command (failed)

> [!WARNING]
> failed to execute command: No such file or directory (os error 2)

## Invalid directives remain visible

An executable is required, unknown directive flags are rejected, and the old
`:sh` spelling is not an alias.

    :shell -q

> [!WARNING]
> Parser error: error: the following required arguments were not provided:
>   <ARGS>...
>
> Usage: :shell --quiet <ARGS>...
>
> For more information, try '--help'.
>

    :$ --unknown echo nope

> [!WARNING]
> Parser error: error: unexpected argument '--unknown' found
>
>   tip: to pass '--unknown' as a value, use '-- --unknown'
>
> Usage: :shell [OPTIONS] <ARGS>...
>
> For more information, try '--help'.
>

    :sh echo unsupported

> [!WARNING]
> Parser error: unknown directive ':sh'

